    <patterns>
      <pattern name="Ordered eligibility refusals" location="src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/Api/ExternalAccess/InternalShareEndpoints.cs:662-683">
        One method returning the first applicable refusal or null, each branch fail-closed on an unreadable value. Retire the third branch without disturbing the order or the fail direction of the first two.
      </pattern>
      <pattern name="Contract-vs-behaviour test split" location="tests/integration/contract/Api/ExternalAccess/InternalUserShareContractTests.cs:1-9">
        The contract tests assert routes, status codes, JSON names and reason codes as LITERALS; the behaviour lives in `tests/integration/auth/UnifiedAccessControl/InternalUserShareTests`. A rule change touches BOTH layers — the header at :1-9 says so explicitly.
      </pattern>
    </patterns>
  </knowledge>

  <steps mode="directional">
    <step order="0">Read the three code sites and both test sites before editing, and settle the two decisions the ruling leaves open: what happens on `IsExternal == null` now that the flag is irrelevant here, and whether the reason-code constant is deleted or retained unused. Answer the escalation below if the licence question is not resolvable from the ruling.</step>
    <step order="1">Remove the refusal branch (`:677-680`) and fix the prose in the same pass: the class doc (`:44-48`), the constant's summary (`:79`) if retained, and whether `sprk_isexternal` is still worth selecting at `:117` given its doc's own warning that every extra column is one whose absence or column-security would 400 the entire read.</step>
    <step order="2">Change BOTH test sites to assert the new rule — an external licensed person now receives a share — and keep the negative cases that must still refuse. Do not simply delete the retired tests: invert them, so the new rule is pinned as explicitly as the old one was.</step>
    <step order="3">Verify the flag's other consumer is untouched: the Communication fan-out seam tests stay green, and `ISystemUserIdentityResolver` is unchanged.</step>
    <step order="4">Build, full suite, ArchTests, publish size versus a fresh master worktree; update the task-063 notes; write this task's notes; report. Leave TASK-INDEX to the main session.</step>
  </steps>

  <escalation>
    <trigger>🔔 The owner's discriminator is LICENCE, and this path reads no licence signal at all — `SystemUserSelect` (`:117`) carries none, and nothing in `src/**` reads `islicensed` or `caltype`. After the branch is removed, eligibility is "exists + enabled + is a person (access mode 0–2, no application id)", which is a PROXY for "licensed security principal", not a licence check. Confirm that proxy satisfies the ruling before shipping. Do NOT invent a licence read — adding a column to this read is a new 400 risk and a wider change than the ruling authorises. If the owner intends an actual licence check, stop and escalate: that is a different task.</trigger>
    <trigger>If `sprk_isexternal` turns out to carry a load-bearing distinction BEYOND the messaging use verified here — an unlicensed guest, an app user, a stub row — STOP. The ruling is about LICENSED users and does not authorise removing a guard that separates something else. The Dataverse MCP server is DOWN this session (`CONNECTION_CLOSED`), so live metadata cannot be consulted; the code-level answer (a custom Spaarke two-option column, default internal, consumed by the communication fan-out) is in `&lt;context&gt;`. If a live check is required to settle it, that is an escalation, not a guess.</trigger>
  </escalation>

  <outputs>
    <output type="code">src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/Api/ExternalAccess/InternalShareEndpoints.cs</output>
    <output type="test">tests/integration/contract/Api/ExternalAccess/InternalUserShareContractTests.cs and tests/integration/auth/UnifiedAccessControl/InternalUserShareTests.cs (KEEP paths: endpoint-contract, security-auth)</output>
    <output type="doc">projects/unified-access-control-r2/notes/task-114-external-licensed-user-share.md, plus the corrections to notes/task-063-internal-user-share-endpoints.md</output>
  </outputs>

  <acceptance-criteria>
    <criterion testable="true">A licensed, enabled, person-access-mode system user with `sprk_isexternal = true` receives a POA share at the requested level: 200, the level echoed, the stored rights mask read back, and outcome `created`.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Both retired test sites now assert the NEW rule rather than being deleted — the contract test at `InternalUserShareContractTests.cs:79-95` and the `[Theory]` at `InternalUserShareTests.cs:430-441`, including its `null` case.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">The `IsExternal == null` outcome matches the decision recorded in step 0 and is pinned by a test, so the retirement of a fail-closed guard is explicit rather than incidental.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Negative case — still refused: a DISABLED user is 422 `user_disabled`, and nothing is written.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Negative case — still refused: an application, support or delegated-admin account (application id set, or access mode outside 0–2) is 422 `user_not_a_person`, and nothing is written — including when it is ALSO flagged external, so the removal of one branch does not let a non-person through.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Negative case — still refused: an unknown user is 404 `user_not_found`, and an unreadable systemuser read is 422/500 `read_failed` rather than a silent success.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Authorization unchanged: the caller still may grant only what they hold — a caller whose own rights do not cover the level gets 403 `caller_cannot_grant`, and the narrowing behaviour is unaffected by this change.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">No refusal path remains reachable for the retired rule: if the reason-code constant is retained it is provably unused, and if deleted no reference survives in code or tests.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">The flag's other consumer is intact: `ISystemUserIdentityResolver`/`IsExternalAsync` is unchanged, and the Communication fan-out seam tests — including the external-licensed-user exclusion at `FanOutTargetingSecuritySeamTests.cs:249-255` — stay green.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Unshare is unaffected: a share held by a disabled, external account is still removable (`InternalUserShareTests.cs:558-566` stays green), since the unshare path reads only existence.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">No prose anywhere states the retired rule: the class doc (`:44-48`), the constant's summary (`:79`) if retained, and `notes/task-063-internal-user-share-endpoints.md` (`:133`, `:355`) all read consistently with the new rule.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Build green; full suite green; ArchTests green; publish size reported against a fresh master worktree and ≤60 MB.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Cover the stated contract plus the edge cases named above. Anything beyond that needs a one-line justification in task notes.</criterion>
  </acceptance-criteria>

  <notes>Authored 2026-09-19. Premises re-verified against HEAD. The owner's ruling and the test hazard are confirmed; FOUR corrections to the register and the earlier stub.

**VERIFIED TRUE** — the refusal exists and is reachable, and the contract test at `InternalUserShareContractTests.cs:94` does assert the retired reason code (test `PostShareUser_ForAnExternalUser_Returns422WithTheReasonCode` at `:80`, seeded `isExternal: true` at `:82`, status at `:93`). "Ship the test with the code" is correct and is the main hazard. No client consumes the reason code (`src/client/**` — nothing).

**CORRECTION 1 — `InternalShareEndpoints.cs:80` is NOT the refusal.** It is the reason-code constant declaration. The refusal is `:677-680`, the third branch of `EligibilityRefusal` (`:666-683`). Both the register (`defer-issues.md:793`) and the earlier stub inherited the wrong line. This matters: deleting `:80` is not the change, and an executor at `:80` would find a `const` and improvise.

**CORRECTION 2 — `SystemUserIdentityResolver.cs:57` does not justify this refusal.** `:50-69` is the doc comment on `IsExternalAsync`, and `:55-60` explains why internal-vs-external uses a FLAG rather than record-type inference for internal-only MESSAGE exclusion, feeding `CommunicationAccessContext.IsInternalUser` for notification fan-out and the timeline read path. Different feature; the share path never calls that resolver — it reads `sprk_isexternal` itself via `SystemUserSelect` (`:117`) into its own `SystemUserRow.IsExternal` (`:744-746`). The stub's phrasing ("a doc comment justifying the refusal") would have invited an executor to edit or delete a comment that documents a live, separate, seam-tested rule.

**CORRECTION 3 — there is a SECOND test site.** `tests/integration/auth/UnifiedAccessControl/InternalUserShareTests.cs:430-441`, `Share_WithAUserNotConfirmedInternal_Is422AndWritesNothing`, a `[Theory]` with `[InlineData(true)]` and `[InlineData(null)]` asserting `(422, InternalShareEndpoints.UserNotInternalReasonCode)` at `:439`. The register's "asserted by a contract test" is incomplete. A reason-code string grep misses it because it asserts through the constant — which is exactly how a "green suite" could be reached while one site still encodes the retired rule.

**CORRECTION 4 — the owner's discriminator is not read on this path.** No licence column is selected (`:117`) and nothing in `src/**` reads `islicensed`/`caltype`. Post-change eligibility is a proxy, not a licence check. Raised as the first escalation trigger rather than silently accepted.

Also verified and useful: the `null` case currently refuses (`user.IsExternal is not false`) and is explicitly pinned, so deleting the branch retires a fail-closed guard — a decision, not a side effect. The fake systemuser table validates `$select` against an allow-list and throws on unknown columns (`InternalUserShareTests.cs:893-900`, `:844`) but projects only what is selected (`:918`), so narrowing `SystemUserSelect` is test-safe.

Related: tasks 060, 061, 063, 065 (the "+ User" picker consumes this surface), 069 (Phase-4 seam tests).</notes>

